  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe cold NW flow pattern fostering scattered on and off light\nrain showers today will be a distant memory soon as a large\npattern shift toward eastern CONUS ridging is likely by the\nstart of the next work week. This results in area temperature\nrising from approximately 10-15 degrees below normal today to\n15-20 degrees above normal by Monday; users should note that\nwhile current NBM model means suggest high probabilities for 90+\ndegree maxT readings that could break daily records, the bias-\ncorrection occurring to its output is likely skewing 5-7 degrees\ntoo warm when compared to the raw output of numerous ensemble\nmodels (favoring more mid to upper 80s). Even accounting for\nthis correction, a rapid shift to upper 80s (and near 90 heat\nindices) Sunday through Tuesday could create minor heat-related\nimpacts mainly in urban areas. Individuals with outdoor\nplans/activities should plan safe heat practices like extra\nhydration and occasional breaks from physical activities.\n\nThe one caveat to these temperatures is that variability in \narea cloud cover and convective evolutions (mainly Sat/Sun/Tue) \ncould cause observed readings to fall just short of forecasted \nvalues. Clarity on those impacts will be better resolved in the\ndays ahead.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLatest ensemble guidance favors two notable periods with\nshortwave movement fostering increased thunderstorm potential\nfor the upper Ohio River Valley: Saturday into Sunday, and\nTuesday into Wednesday. Given the previously mentioned\ntemperature rises, any such movement could lend to some non-zero\nsevere threat pending greater understanding of smaller scale\nfeatures (of which is not available at this time). Examination\nof a few publicly available ML/AI models agrees that this period\ncould trend toward having a severe threat of some magnitude if\nthose details align to maximize buoyancy and lift. Stay tuned\nas future model runs should offer greater insight on the\nvalidity of severe potential for either of these periods.",
